From 854792cbc6a42a3772e813c92c314b9c010a7c81 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andre Marin <andre@alexamarin.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 20:42:52 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] fix(pipeline): read decompose as unfinished while its planned
 issues have no key
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`delivery.md` existing was the whole check, and that file is written before the
issues exist by design — creating them is outward-facing and waits for a human.
So the state the sentinel called finished was the normal middle of the step, and
since #148 `implement` advances unattended: a run flowed straight into building
PRs that had no issue to close.

Read from the file's own text rather than the tracker. `wfctl status` runs on
every session start, and a pipeline read that costs a network round-trip is one
nobody makes — the argument `wfctl-runs-the-verification` already uses against
shelling out to the test suite on every status.

The key is matched in the `Issue` cell, not across the row.
`speckit-orchestrate` searches whole rows because it is matching one key it
already holds; here `Closes With` carries `PR #4`, which would answer for the
`_(TBD)_` beside it and hand back the exact state this check exists to catch.

The shape comes from the tracker's `key_pattern`, so `PROJ-4` counts as created
where a hardcoded `\d+` would have called every non-GitHub repo's plan unfinished
forever. A delivery plan with no Issue Grouping Map still reads done: the absence
of a table is not evidence that issues are missing.

Closes #8

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
